findings.what:
RECHECK OF THE INSERTION-HEDGE PREMISE. I-297 is in-plan. The round-12 judge refuted I-298 because '#38 hedges the larger uncertainty, whose failure drops us below the ~350 exact copies'. New field data change what that failure costs. The #38 twin (= X + eps) pays for a medal only if all three hold:
(i) the insertion is negative on private: P 0.10-0.33;
(ii) lowrefine's eps > 0: P ~0.35-0.5 (F-MOD-17: ~1e-5, coin-flip sign, possibly exactly 0);
(iii) X+ itself ranks inside bronze: P ~0-0.35 by r16fc-2.
Value: ~0.23 x 0.4 x 0.15 ~ 0.014 P(bronze), upper bound ~0.33 x 0.5 x 0.35 ~ 0.06. In the grown field, a file just above X gets no medal in most worlds, so for the medal it makes no difference whether we fall below the copies or sit just above them. Compare a second file that KEEPS #37's insertion and adds an independent upside term u, i.e. #37 + u (e.g. a DC or fm45 variant). It is worth E[max(u,0)] in the I > 0 worlds (~77%), which is +0.02..+0.15 P(bronze) depending on sd(u). This is not a new mechanism and not a re-proposal of I-298 for class A/B. It is a changed input to decisions already on the table: class-C option (b) and the user's pair choice. The user decides; no rule changes without the user.
